##  Business Problem
A pizza restaurant wants to understand **what sells, when it sells and what earns the most**, so it can plan staffing, menu and promotions.

##  Dataset
Four related CSV files covering **1 Jan to 31 Dec 2015**.

| Table | Rows | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `orders` | 21,350 | One row per order (date and time) |
| `order_details` | 48,620 | One row per pizza line in an order (pizza and quantity) |
| `pizzas` | 96 | Each pizza by size, with price |
| `pizza_types` | 32 | Pizza name, category and ingredients |

## 🗄 Database Schema
```
pizza_types (pizza_type_id PK, name, category, ingredients)
     │ 1
     │
     ▼ N
pizzas (pizza_id PK, pizza_type_id FK, size, price)
     │ 1
     │
     ▼ N
order_details (order_details_id PK, order_id FK, pizza_id FK, quantity)
     ▲ N
     │
     │ 1
orders (order_id PK, date, time)
```

##  Data Quality Checks
| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Order lines with a pizza_id missing from `pizzas` | **0** |
| NULL dates or times in `orders` | **0** |
| Date range | 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 (358 trading days) |
| Price range | $9.75 to $35.95 (average $16.44) |

> **Note:** `price` must be `DECIMAL(6,2)`. Using `INT` rounds prices such as $12.75 and produces wrong revenue.

